00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:27,740
Conversation 2. did your annual performance evaluation meeting with your manager go?

43
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:29,840
Did you get much in the way of praise?

44
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:31,360
Next to zero.

45
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:36,940
When we came to the part about discussing my areas for growth, he bluntly told me

46
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:38,800
that I had an attitude problem.

47
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:41,840
But he wouldn't really expand on that description.

48
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,680
He said it's the little things I do and say.

49
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,680
you ask him for a specific example?

50
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,880
I did ask him a few more questions to try to narrow it down.

51
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:57,460
He said my constant questioning of him and his decisions was the most obvious

52
00:02:57,460 --> 00:02:58,500
example he could give.

53
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,140
Then our conversation was swiftly brought to a close.

54
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:04,920
I had a similar experience once.

55
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:07,460
My manager told me I was too honest.

56
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:12,080
In most cases, I thought my honesty was helpful, as did my colleagues.

57
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:14,220
But my manager thought otherwise.

58
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,840
Some managers really adopt manipulative language to disguise the unreasonable

59
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,140
choices they have made.

60
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:27,300
They don't use the rational power of arguments to resolve issues of conflict or

61
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:29,120
complaints about unfairness.

62
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:35,640
Right. Some employ their persuasive vocabulary and influential communication style

63
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:40,680
to win workplace arguments by compelling people to perform the intended action.

64
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:43,280
Exactly. Too honest?

65
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:44,760
Isn't that a good thing?

66
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:49,040
I thought that's surely something we should have covered earlier in the section

67
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:51,400
discussing strengths and talents.

68
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:57,140
But no. Honesty was deemed to be more of a sin than a virtue, since it made people

69
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,080
feel uncomfortable.

70
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,240
Or at least it made the most powerful folks feel uncomfortable.

71
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:07,980
Indeed. Rhetoric is viewed by many philosophers as the method through which the

72
00:04:07,980 --> 00:04:10,280
powerful accumulate more power.

73
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:16,160
By virtue of having the loudest voice, they're able to command greater attention and

74
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:20,560
to further assert their dominance, even when what they're saying doesn't really make

75
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:25,260
much sense. And retaining their power and prestige is their priority.

76
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:28,680
It's what makes the most sense to a lot of them.

77
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:33,880
